---
/**
 * The hero — one viewport tall, with everything inside it.
 * See docs/hero.md.
 *
 * Vertical structure is a three-row grid, not a flex column with justify-end:
 * flex pushed the whole block to the bottom and left a dead field above the
 * headline. The rows are nav / copy / stage, and only the stage row flexes, so
 * the copy keeps its position near the top at any viewport height.
 *
 * The stage row is `minmax(0, 1fr)` and the frame inside it is `h-full`, so the
 * stage takes exactly the height that is left over and never a pixel more. The
 * hero used to crop the stage against the section's bottom edge as a scroll
 * cue; the maintainer retired that on 2026-08-29 — the whole hero has to be
 * readable in one viewport, with clear air under the frame.
 *
 * Spacing above the copy is in `svh`, so the proportions hold on a short laptop
 * screen and a tall monitor alike.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Container from "@/components/Container.astro";
import DemoStage from "@/components/demo/DemoStage";
import SectionTrack from "@/components/SectionTrack.astro";
import backdrop from "@/assets/hero/stage-backdrop.webp";

// The frame is the `content` step, so on a wide screen the backdrop only
// ever needs that column's width. Kept out of the markup because the style
// gate reads `width:` in a media condition as a content width.
const backdropSizes = "(min-width: 1400px) 1280px, 100vw"; // layout-allow: an <img sizes> fetch hint, not a layout width
---

<!--
  ONE VIEWPORT MINUS A TAIL, NOT EXACTLY ONE.

  A boundary on this page is ONE line, and it belongs to the section BELOW:
  LogoStrip opens with the `.section-rule` that closes this hero. At exactly
  `100svh` that line lands on the fold's own pixel row and the window edge eats
  it, so the first screen opened with a rule under the nav and had nothing to
  answer it at the bottom — the page just ran out from under the reader.

  Holding the section one `--space-xxl` short pulls that same line up into the
  screen with air beneath it. The line is still drawn by the section below, and
  there is still only one of them (maintainer, 2026-08-29).

  WHY NOT A CLOSING RULE INSIDE THE HERO. A second `.section-rule` as the last
  child would draw the hero's own bottom line — and LogoStrip's opening rule
  would still draw its own a few dozen pixels lower. Two lines at one boundary
  is the exact thing the single-rule convention exists to prevent, and moving
  the section's own edge costs no element at all.
-->
<section
  class="grid min-h-[calc(100svh_-_var(--space-xxl))] grid-rows-[auto_minmax(0,1fr)] overflow-hidden"
>
  <!--
    Nav sits on the `full` step: background edge to edge, content inside.

    Its bottom edge is not a `border-b` on the band any more: that border ran
    the full width of the window, the one horizontal line on the page that did
    not start and end on a rail. It is a `.section-rule` in a Container instead,
    so it spans exactly the two rails like every other section boundary and
    gives the marker a corner to start from (maintainer, 2026-08-29).

    IT HAS TO LIVE INSIDE THIS BAND, not at the top of the hero's box below.
    Drawn there it is invisible: `h-16` is 4rem against a root size that scales
    with the viewport, so the band's bottom edge lands on a FRACTION of a device
    pixel (75.83px at 1.5x), and a sticky opaque band is a composited layer that
    snaps its own edge up to the next whole one — straight over a hairline that
    begins exactly where it ends. Inside the band the line is painted by the
    same layer and nothing can round over it. It also keeps the line pinned
    under the nav for as long as the nav is pinned, which is what the eye
    expects of a band with an edge.
  -->
  <header class="sticky top-0 z-30 h-16 bg-canvas relative">
    <Container width="content" class="flex h-16 items-center justify-between">
      <span class="text-[18px] font-semibold text-ink">Personal Jarvis</span>
      <nav class="flex items-center gap-7 text-[14px] font-medium text-body">
        <a href="#features" class="hover:text-ink">Features</a>
        <a href="#docs" class="hover:text-ink">Docs</a>
        <a
          href="#install"
          class="rounded-full bg-ink px-5 py-2.5 text-on-ink transition-colors hover:bg-ink-active"
        >
          Download
        </a>
      </nav>
    </Container>

    <Container width="content" class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0">
      <div class="section-rule"></div>
    </Container>
  </header>

  <!--
    THE HERO'S OWN BOX — everything under the nav. It is a separate element and
    not the `<section>` because the section's top edge is the top of the NAV,
    and a marker starting there would begin its walk behind an opaque sticky
    band. This box starts on the rule instead, which is the corner the reader
    can actually see. `SectionTrack` measures the box it is dropped into, so
    naming the box is all it takes.
  -->
  <div class="relative grid min-h-0 grid-rows-[auto_minmax(0,1fr)]">
    <SectionTrack />

    <div class="pt-[5svh]">
      <Container width="prose">
        <!--
          Capped well below the old 6rem: the headline is the one element that
          decides whether the stage still has room, and three lines of it is the
          shape the maintainer asked for (2026-08-29).
        -->
        <h1
          class="text-center text-balance leading-[1.08] tracking-[-1.5px]"
          style="font-size: clamp(1.875rem, 1.3rem + 1.5vw, 3.25rem)"
        >
          Speak to your computer and it does the work
        </h1>

        <div class="mt-7 flex flex-col justify-center gap-3 sm:flex-row">
          <a
            href="#install"
            class="inline-flex min-h-[44px] items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-ink px-6 text-[15px] font-medium text-on-ink transition-colors hover:bg-ink-active"
          >
            Download for Windows
          </a>
          <a
            href="#docs"
            class="inline-flex min-h-[44px] items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-hairline-strong bg-card px-6 text-[15px] font-medium text-ink transition-colors hover:border-ink"
          >
            Read the docs
          </a>
        </div>
      </Container>
    </div>

    <!--
      The stage row. `min-h-0` lets the row shrink past its content's intrinsic
      height — without it the grid track grows to fit the board and the hero
      spills past the fold again.

      The padding is 6svh and not the 10svh it was, because the band under the
      frame is now built from two pieces rather than one: this padding, then the
      section's 48px tail below the closing rule. Together they come to the same
      distance the old single padding did, so the frame keeps the height it had
      — the band beneath it simply gained a line partway down.
    -->
    <Container width="content" class="min-h-0 pt-6 pb-[6svh]">
      <!--
        `hairline-strong`, not the usual hairline (kept from 09943cb): the frame
        now holds a pale painting on a near-black page, and the stronger edge is
        what stops that bright rectangle from bleeding into the floor around it.
        The reason has changed with the backdrop; the choice has not.
      -->
      <div
        class="hero-frame relative h-full min-h-[300px] overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-hairline-strong"
      >
        <!--
          The backdrop. An original painting generated for this site, so there is
          no third-party licence attached to it — see docs/hero.md, "Backdrop".
          Eager and above the fold; `object-cover` because the frame's own ratio
          moves with the viewport height.
        -->
        <Image
          src={backdrop}
          alt=""
          widths={[1280, 1920, 2560]}
          sizes={backdropSizes}
          loading="eager"
          class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover"
        />
        <DemoStage client:load className="relative h-full" />
      </div>
    </Container>
  </div>
</section>

<style is:global>
  /*
   * `<astro-island>` is an unknown element, so it computes to `display: inline`
   * and carries no height of its own. The stage inside it asks for
   * `height: 100%`, which against an inline parent resolves to auto — the stage
   * then falls back to its own aspect ratio and stops fitting the frame. On a
   * short viewport that puts the bottom of the window outside the frame, which
   * is the one thing this hero is built to prevent.
   *
   * `is:global` because the wrapper is generated by the hydration runtime and
   * never carries this component's scope attribute.
   */
  .hero-frame > astro-island {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
  }
</style>
